// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: properties, purity, soundness, computational-properties, builtins
// @expected-error: E503
// @expected-error-count: 3
//
// Pin (T0792, first part) — printing inside a `pure fn` is a side effect and
// must be reported.
//
// Property inference already had the machinery: the call arm looks the callee
// up in a registry of known function properties.  The registry was NEVER
// POPULATED — its `register_function` had no caller anywhere in the tree — so
// every lookup returned nothing and every callee was taken to be pure.  A
// `pure fn` that printed was therefore accepted in silence, which is the worst
// shape a defect can take: the compiler was verifying a promise it had stopped
// reading.
//
// The printers are seeded into that registry because their effect cannot be
// discovered from a Verum body — they have none, the work happens in the
// runtime.  That is the whole of what this pin covers.  Properties travelling
// from a USER function to its callers is a separate mechanism and is not
// claimed here; `purity_allows_pure_wrappers.vr` and the file below hold the
// line on what must stay quiet.
//
// The third case is a printer behind a wrapper, and it needs both halves of
// the work to fail correctly: T0791 made the wrapper transparent, and this
// change gave the callee a property worth carrying.  Either one alone leaves
// it silent.

pure fn prints() -> Int {
    print("x");
    2
}

pure fn prints_a_line() -> Int {
    println("x");
    2
}

pure fn prints_behind_a_wrapper() -> Int {
    (print("x"));
    2
}

fn main() {
    print(prints() + prints_a_line() + prints_behind_a_wrapper());
}
